<main id="main">
<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-ph" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Common questions</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Answers</p><h1>Common Questions About Insurance Appraisal in Minnesota</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers grouped by topic — the basics, costs, timing, Minnesota rules and working with us.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-sky" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="jump" aria-label="Question topics"><a href="#q-0">Basics</a><a href="#q-1">Costs &amp; fees</a><a href="#q-2">Timing &amp; deadlines</a><a href="#q-3">Minnesota rules</a><a href="#q-4">Working with us</a></nav><h2 id="q-0">Basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What does an insurance appraiser actually do?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>An insurance appraiser values the cost to repair or replace damaged property under the terms of a policy, then negotiates that amount with the other side’s appraiser. The goal is a signed award that sets the amount of loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>How is appraisal different from going to court?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal is a narrower, policy-based process that decides only the dollar amount of a covered loss. Lawsuits handle coverage, contract and bad-faith questions, and they usually take longer and cost more.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can the insurance company demand appraisal too?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. The provision usually lets either party make a written demand. If the insurer invokes it, you still choose your own appraiser.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the appraisal award final?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>On the amount of loss, an award signed by two of the three panel members is generally binding. Courts can set awards aside only in narrow situations, such as fraud or a panel exceeding its authority.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal work for commercial buildings?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Most commercial property forms contain an appraisal provision, and the process for apartment buildings, churches, retail and industrial properties follows the same basic steps.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-1">Costs &amp; fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Each side usually pays its own appraiser, and the umpire’s fee is commonly split equally. Check your policy, because some forms allocate costs differently.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do appraisers charge?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Arrangements vary: hourly, flat fee, or a combination. Because appraisers must be disinterested, many avoid contingency fees tied to the size of the award. Get the fee agreement in writing before you sign anything.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small gap?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often not. If the difference is only a few thousand dollars, appraisal fees can eat most of it, and a well-supported supplement request may be the better route. Our claim gap fee estimator can help you run illustrative numbers.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are there costs besides the appraiser and umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Sometimes. Specialized input such as an engineer’s report, a contractor’s detailed bid or a matching test for siding may add cost, depending on the dispute.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-2">Timing &amp; deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take in Minnesota?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many residential appraisals finish within two to four months, but timing depends on the size of the loss, scheduling, winter access to roofs and how many items reach the umpire.</p></div></details><details><summary>How quickly must I name my appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies allow about 20 days after the written demand. Read your own appraisal paragraph, because the window can differ.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal stop the lawsuit deadline?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not necessarily. Standard policy language commonly limits suits to two years after the loss, and appraisal may not pause that period. Confirm the deadline in your policy and with an attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal happen in winter?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, though roof inspections may wait for safe conditions. Interior damage from ice dams can usually be inspected and priced right away.</p></div></details><details><summary>When is recoverable depreciation paid after an award?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation once repairs are completed and documented. Your policy sets the deadline for completing repairs and claiming it.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-3">Minnesota rules</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Where does Minnesota’s appraisal provision come from?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Minnesota’s standard fire policy statute, Minn. Stat. § 65A.01, includes an appraisal provision with two competent and disinterested appraisers, an umpire, and an award by any two. Most policies sold here follow that structure.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisers decide what caused the damage in Minnesota?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>In Quade v. Secura Insurance (2012), the Minnesota Supreme Court indicated that appraisers may decide causation as part of the amount of loss. Coverage questions still belong to the courts.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in Minnesota?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The Minnesota Department of Commerce oversees insurance companies and licenses agents, adjusters and public adjusters. It also accepts consumer complaints.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if I can’t get coverage from a regular insurer?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The Minnesota FAIR Plan provides basic property coverage when the voluntary market won’t. Minnesota has no separate state wind pool, so wind and hail are typically covered in the regular policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who appoints an umpire if the appraisers can’t agree?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Either party can ask a court to appoint one, typically the district court in the county where the property is located.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-4">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are you lawyers or public adjusters?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. We are independent appraisers. We value the amount of loss and serve as appraisers or umpires; we don’t give legal advice or negotiate coverage.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I send for a first review?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Your full policy, the carrier’s estimate, any contractor bids, photos and key correspondence. That is usually enough to tell whether appraisal makes sense.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work outside the Twin Cities?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. We handle claims throughout Minnesota, from the metro to Duluth, Rochester, St. Cloud, Mankato and smaller communities.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you serve as an umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, when both parties or a court select us and we have no conflict with either side. Umpire work is strictly neutral.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do I get started?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Use the contact form or call. We’ll ask a few questions about the claim, review your documents and tell you plainly whether appraisal looks like the right tool.</p></div></details></div></article><aside class="sidebar"><div class="sticky"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-t">Talk it through</h2><p>A free first conversation about your claim. No pressure, no obligation.</p><a class="side-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-sky btn-block" href="/contact-us/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm Central</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/appraisal-process-guide/">Appraisal process guide</a></li><li><a href="/common-questions/" aria-current="page">Common questions</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-terms/">Insurance terms</a></li><li><a href="/learning-center/">Learning center</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="cta-strip"><div class="wrap"><div class="cta-strip-in rv"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email — we’re glad to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-sky" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/contact-us/">Send the estimate</a></div></div></div></section>
